  "name": "Stationary Two-Foot Jump Rope",
  "definition": "A continuous in-place basic bounce with a regular unweighted rope, simultaneous two-foot takeoff and landing, and one forward rope passage per jump. Low clearance and synchronized rope turnover distinguish it from height-seeking pogos and discrete countermovement jumps. The squat pattern describes shared bilateral hip-and-knee flexion/extension during shallow repeated jumps; it does not prescribe squat depth or a strength repetition. Mullerpatan supports categorical lower-limb absorption and propulsion in a related bilateral bounce protocol, not the exact clearance, cadence, or joint excursions of this fixture. Calf emphasis, hip/knee secondary roles, trunk control, ankle/foot control, and sustained handle-grip roles are bounded anatomy-and-mechanics inferences, not exact-fixture EMG rankings. Rope turnover is described in instructions without inventing a single wrist action. Power and rep tracking are product conventions for counted jumps; no timed-conditioning, medical, adaptation, or quantitative performance claim is made.",
